Skip dhclient carrier-wait loop when wait=False

dhclient.start() polls /sys/class/net/<iface>/carrier up to 5 times with
1s sleeps before launching dhclient, regardless of the `wait` argument.
For callers that pass wait=False (which translates to dhclient -nw), the
carrier loop is pointless: -nw already tells dhclient to background and
retry on its own, so the up-to-5s preamble is pure overhead.

This is a real regression for SONiC ZTP boot. With dhcp-wait="no" set in
the ifupdown2 dhcp policy, every inband port hits the full 5s timeout
during start-networking because port carrier is 0 at that point (swss
and syncd are still coming up). On a 64-port platform that adds ~5
minutes (64 * 5s) to the networking restart, regressing ZTP boot from
~30s to ~6min.

Gate the carrier loop on `wait`. When wait=True the existing behaviour
is preserved; when wait=False the loop is skipped and dhclient is
started immediately with -nw.

Verified on a 64-port NH-5010 ZTP boot:
  - Pre-patch:  Ethernet0 stop -> dhclient -nw 5050ms; full restart 5m49s
  - Post-patch: Ethernet0 stop -> dhclient -nw   38ms; full restart 38s

---
 ifupdown2/ifupdownaddons/dhclient.py | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ifupdown2/ifupdownaddons/dhclient.py b/ifupdown2/ifupdownaddons/dhclient.py
--- a/ifupdown2/ifupdownaddons/dhclient.py
+++ b/ifupdown2/ifupdownaddons/dhclient.py
@@ -69,8 +69,8 @@ class dhclient(utilsBase):
         retries = 0
         out = "0"

-        # wait if interface isn't up yet
-        while '1' not in out and retries < 5:
+        # wait if interface isn't up yet (skip when wait=False, i.e. dhcp-wait=no)
+        while wait and '1' not in out and retries < 5:
             path = '/sys/class/net/%s/carrier' %ifacename
             out = self.read_file_oneline(path)
             if out is None:
